  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nActive weather is expected Friday and Saturday as multiple \ntroughs interact to bring in Pacific moisture. A deepening low \npressure system will begin to form over the eastern plains of \nColorado and Wyoming Friday. There is a lot of uncertainty with \nhow this system will unfold. Models are indicating steep low-level\nlapse rates leading to highly variable precipitation and snowfall\namounts across relatively short distances. One example highlighted\nby high resolution models is the placement of a heavy\nprecipitation band of 0.50+ inches of precipitation northwest of \nthe low pressure system. Some models (RRFS) are keeping this band\nin Northeast Wyoming/Western South Dakota, while other models\n(HRRR) take this band from Sheridan through Baker. These \ndifferences would lead to significant changes in snowfall amounts.\n\nMost locations can expect 0-0.20 inches of precipitation. The\ngreatest confidence in lower end values is an area north of \nMelstone to Forsyth to Plevna where little to no precipitation is \nexpected. The best chance (40-90%) for getting greater than 0.25 \ninches of precipitation is along the mountains and foothills. \nPrecipitation is expected to be mostly snow, with the potential \nfor a rain/snow mix during the day Friday. The Friday evening\ncommute could be impacted by wintry driving conditions. Snowfall \namounts could exceed 6 inches for northwest facing slopes in \nmountain locations.\n\nThe coldest temperatures of the fall are expected through Tuesday\nwith high temperatures generally in the 30s F and lows in the\nteens and twenties. Temperatures are expected to warm throughout\nnext week with high temperatures returning to the 40s and\npotentially even 50s F. Dry conditions are expected next week. \nTorgerson",
